# Scheduling inputs for balance.sh --queue.
#
#   build <target> <seconds>   compile cost, roughly node-independent
#   cache <target> <bytes>     layer cache the target exports
#
# The third term, each node's upload throughput to the store, is no
# longer here: it describes the fleet rather than a target, so it is
# `link_mbps:` on the node in manifests/<fleet>.yml. balance.sh reads
# it through fleet.py and charges it in the same `netmb` row format
# this file used, so the arithmetic below is unchanged.
#
# Two terms, because a target's cost is not one number. The build is
# CPU work; the cache export is a transfer, and the fleet's links
# differ by a factor of 25. Scheduling on build time alone put
# triton-wheel - 6.08GB of cache, the largest of any target - on the
# only node behind WiFi, where the upload took ~28 minutes against the
# ~4 minutes it took to build. That one placement was most of a
# normal build's wall clock.
#
# Measured 2026-10-29: build times from run 20261029T204149Z (gfx1201,
# cold, cache export cleared); link speeds from a 200MB HTTP PUT to the
# store from each node; transfer bytes from the HTTP spans of run
# 20261029T230457Z (gfx1201, normal).
#
# The bytes come from a *normal* run on purpose. The first version of
# this table took them from a cold run's Zot log, which counts the
# cache export but not the image push, because perf-cold passes
# --no-push. That under-counted aiter-wheel by 4.3x - 338MB against a
# real 1450MB - and the scheduler put it on the WiFi node, where its
# export alone ran 8m24s against a 2m39s build. Gaming's four targets
# then needed 12.5 minutes of upload between them and became the
# critical path of a 26m run, while snoc-strix sat idle after 55s.
#
# pytorch-wheel keeps its cold-run figure: it was a near-total cache
# hit in that run and transferred 17KB, which is what a target costs
# when it does not build, not what it costs when it does.
#
# Estimates for ordering, not promises. A stale table degrades the
# schedule, never the build. Re-measure after anything that moves the
# build materially - and re-measure each node's link_mbps in the fleet
# manifest if the fleet is rewired, since that is the term that now
# dominates.

build pytorch-wheel   2336
build vllm-wheel      1409
build triton-wheel     988
build cupy-wheel       182
build nixl-wheel       180
build aiter-wheel       72
build lmcache-wheel     66

# Measured 2026-11-04, run 20261104T164632Z (gfx1201). Build work only:
# the 18 kind=step spans sum to 25.0s and the last completes 26.6s after
# the first starts. The placeholder here was 400, guessed high on the
# theory that ~25 HIP files would dominate; they do not. Only 7 are HIP
# (ops/hip/*.hip), the rest are C++, and most of even this is apt, the
# shallow clone, pip and the verification steps rather than compilation.
#
# NOT that run's reported_seconds, which is 75.4s. The difference is
# 48.8s of registry transfer, and putting it in this row would be wrong
# twice over. The cold run that produced every other build row,
# 20261029T204149Z, carries no transfer spans at all — perf-cold passes
# --no-push — so those rows are pure build work and 75 would not be
# comparable to them. And cost_of already charges the export separately
# as c/(n*1048576) from the cache row below, so the 48.8s would be
# counted a second time, on a link it was not even measured over.
#
# That is a trap for anyone re-measuring from a warm run: reported_seconds
# is the whole vertex span, push included. Take the kind=step spans.
build torchvision-wheel  27

# PLACEHOLDER, not measured. ATOM compiles no C++ and no HIP — the only
# compile in it is the atomesh cargo build — so this should sit well
# below the HIP targets. 120s is a guess that deliberately errs high:
# an over-estimate costs a good node on one target, an under-estimate
# costs the scheduler its ordering entirely (see the pytorch story
# below). Replace from a cold run: `make perf-cold` then `make
# perf-report`, taking the kind=step spans as the header describes.
build atom-wheel        120

# The image targets. These were absent until 2026-10-30, and the
# absence was not harmless: cost_of falls back to build=1 for a target
# with no row, so the scheduler believed `pytorch` cost one second.
# ready_targets sorts by cost descending and the packer is LPT, so the
# single most expensive target in the graph sorted LAST and was handed
# the weakest node — measured on run 20261030T171813Z, where pytorch
# took 3388s on snoc-gaming while pytorch-wheel did the same compile in
# 933s on snoc-think. Nothing warned; a missing row and a genuinely
# one-second target are indistinguishable to awk.
#
# These are weaker numbers than the rows above, and deliberately
# marked as such. `make build-status` cannot separate pytorch from
# pytorch-wheel, nor vllm from vllm-wheel, because each pair shares a
# BUILDKIT_BUILD_NAME (rocm-bake.hcl:593 and :980) — so these come
# from reasoning plus observed spans, not from a clean per-target
# measurement.
#
#   pytorch       equal to pytorch-wheel: the two are `final` and
#                 `wheel` over ONE builder stage, so cold they do
#                 identical work. Corroborated by the 38m25s/38m33s
#                 pair in buildx history. Note the scheduler now
#                 serialises them, so whichever runs second is a near
#                 total cache hit (68.8s measured) — this row is the
#                 cost of the one that runs FIRST, which is what
#                 placement has to be right about.
#   vllm          same argument against vllm-wheel; 23m15s/23m19s
#                 observed, consistent with vllm-wheel's 1409.
#   lmcache       1m45s/1m49s/1m42s observed across three nodes.
#   pytorch-base  5s/6s observed. Small, but a row of 6 beats a
#                 fallback of 1 that means "unknown".
#   pytorch-src   1260 = a measured 1204s clone plus ~56s of apt and
#                 export, from the 2026-11-03 gfx1201 build. Almost
#                 entirely network: it fetches PyTorch and every
#                 submodule, and compiles nothing. Place it anywhere
#                 with bandwidth, not anywhere with cores — and note
#                 it is built once per PYTORCH_VERSION, so most runs
#                 never schedule it at all.
#   rocm-runtime  NOT YET MEASURED. 300 is a placeholder, chosen high
#                 rather than low: the target purges 260 packages out
#                 of a 20 GB tree and then copies the 4.4 GB that
#                 survives into a fresh layer, which is a lot of I/O
#                 and no compilation. Placed too cheap it would land on
#                 snoc-gaming behind WiFi, and every other target waits
#                 on it. The prune itself is measured at ~13s; what
#                 this row needs is that plus the layer export, which
#                 only a fleet run shows. rocm-runtime is not in the
#                 wheels group, so `make perf-warm` does NOT measure
#                 it — it is built by the bootstrap, ahead of and
#                 outside the measured run. Time `make
#                 push-rocm-runtime` directly instead.
build pytorch         2336
build vllm            1409
build rocm-runtime     300
build pytorch-src     1260
build lmcache          105
build pytorch-base       6

# STALE as of the --revision change in Dockerfile.triton. This figure
# was measured 2026-10-29, when the clone wrote 838MB of unreachable
# history into the exported layer; that is now 8.9MB, so this row
# over-states triton-wheel's export by roughly 829MB. balance.sh packs
# on build + cache/netmb, so until it is re-measured the scheduler
# keeps triton-wheel off nodes it can now afford — snoc-gaming most of
# all, where 829MB is ~224s of upload that no longer happens.
# Re-measure with `make perf-warm` then `make perf-report`, taking the
# transfer bytes from the HTTP spans as the header describes.
cache triton-wheel    6695603582
cache vllm-wheel      2954089155
cache aiter-wheel     1520455242
cache lmcache-wheel    615890669
cache cupy-wheel       402995659
cache nixl-wheel       372131035
cache pytorch-wheel   5046000000
# Measured 2026-11-04, run 20261104T164632Z: 2,595,074,525 bytes over
# 64 uploads, summed from the Content-Length of every PUT/PATCH/POST to
# a torchvision-wheel path in that run's zot.jsonl. Same method the
# header describes.
#
# The placeholder was 400MB, borrowed from cupy-wheel on the theory
# that the two were the closest in shape. They are not: this is 6.2x
# that, and the pairing with a 75s build makes torchvision-wheel the
# most export-heavy-per-second-of-compile target in the table. That is
# precisely the profile the header's triton-wheel story warns about, so
# it is the row most likely to move a placement now that best_node_for
# actually reads these.
cache torchvision-wheel 2595074525

# PARTLY MEASURED. The published image is 20,854,576 bytes over three
# zstd layers, read from the manifest after the first push on
# 2026-11-05: 13,641,288 for the wheel, 7,163,114 for the atomesh
# binary, 50,174 for the logs. That is the image, not the export this
# row costs — cost_of charges the builder stage's cache layers too, and
# those are not in the manifest. 60MB carries the measured image plus
# room for them.
#
# The first placeholder here was 250MB, guessed before anything had
# been built; the image alone came in at a twelfth of that. Still
# re-measure properly the way torchvision's row above was: sum
# Content-Length over every PUT/PATCH/POST to an atom-wheel path in a
# run's zot.jsonl.
cache atom-wheel         60000000
